00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:45.920
Here's a few like a
performance improvement

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.760
highlight I'd like to show you.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.360
Having a clock in a computer
system is important.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.570
It'll be even better if
the clock can be accurate

00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:00.140
and the cost to access
the clock is low.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:02.180
It turns out that
it's rather difficult

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:04.780
to implement accurate
and efficient time

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:09.260
in a virtualized environment.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:13.570
Most of the implementation comes
with some performance cost.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:15.780
The server application
like SQL Server

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.580
do a lot of sampling to
measure the operation latency.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.450
So that's why having an
efficient time implementation

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:26.660
is extremely important for
server workload on a cloud

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.650
environment.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:32.429
[INAUDIBLE] TSC is a
hyper-v environment

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:35.630
which allows the virtual
machine to access clock

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:39.890
with native performance
without any performance loss.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.010
You can also help bring
to keep the clock accurate

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:48.320
even where you might create a
machine among different hosts.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.260
To support these
feature in cloud moment

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:53.490
is much more
difficult to support

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:56.000
this [INAUDIBLE] environment.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.620
We are very proud to be the
first cloud provider to be

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.500
able to support this feature.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:06.070
In our internal testing, where
we ran the TPCC benchmark--

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:09.310
of course, you do a lot of,
like, tunnel excess, right?

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:12.550
We are seeing the CPU usage
can job up to 20% to 30%.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.740
All this preciosu
CPU cycle could

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.630
be used to do
something more useful

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:24.490
rather than waste on
accessing the timer.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:26.900
We also made our storage
stack and the network

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:28.620
stack more efficient.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:32.690
I already talked about the
narrow throughput, the PD SSD.

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:35.730
But also SCSI Multi-Queue
is another feature

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.880
which is super useful
for our local SSD

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.500
offering on the Windows.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:44.980
It made our virtual storage
adapter more efficient,

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.601
with lower latency
and higher throughput.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:55.470
Since we [INAUDIBLE] the
SQL Server Standard Edition

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.980
like last year, a top
question from the customer

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.100
is when Google will start
to support the SQL Server

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.050
enterprise with always
