00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:06.600
So here we are.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.670
Mission critical
SQL server workload

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:12.290
requires higher visibility
and disaster recovery.

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:14.730
To achieve this,
Google Cloud Platform

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.290
supports Windows server
failover cluster.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.424
and always on
availability group.

00:10:19.424 --> 00:10:20.840
Always on availability
great group

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:24.090
is the SQL Server
flagship HADR solution

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.520
allowing you to configure
multiple replica

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:29.270
for automatic failover
in case of failure.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:33.960
This replica can be
configured as readable,

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:37.035
allowing you to offload the
read workload and the backup.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.480
So look at the list
reference architecture.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.060
You needed two custom
subnetworks here.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:51.830
Each custom subnetwork will
host one SQL Server instance.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:55.717
The main controller on top
can be the same or different

00:10:55.717 --> 00:10:56.550
than the subnetwork.

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:58.960
Doesn't matter.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.590
You will also need
Active Directory

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:05.730
as with most typical
windows workload.

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:11.110
And we also promote these domain
controllers to the DNS server.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:15.400
For both SQL instance, we need
to join with Active Directory

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:20.150
and also update the DNS
setting to use the DNS server.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:25.770
That's important but the default
GCE DNS will not work here.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:30.200
In order to specify the
cluster IP and the listen IP

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:33.200
when you're building
out your cluster,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.970
we need to switch from
the DCHP to the static IP

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:41.110
and also config the IP
address using the TCP advance

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.520
[INAUDIBLE] so that
the packet can be

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:46.625
forwarded to the correct node.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.740
So in this reference
architecture on the last slide,

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:56.700
I talk about a lot like
how, how you do that.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.480
So now I want to talk
a little bit of why,

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:00.570
why you want to do that.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:03.500
What's the reason behind that?

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:06.390
First, I would like to point
out that the slide I just

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:10.870
showed you is like an original
[INAUDIBLE] configuration.

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:14.400
However, you can configure
two subnets in the [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:18.400
or even configure two different
subnet across different regions

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:21.600
to make this a cross region
[INAUDIBLE] configuration.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.550
Of course, if it
is cross region,

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.820
you may want to configure
your SQL application

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.030
using asynchronous syncrhonous
replication instead

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:34.890
of synchronous replication to
mitigate the latency issue.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.470
Second, the whole
solution was building

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:40.680
on top of a multi-site
failover cluster.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.170
Because it's multi-site,
that's the reason

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:47.550
we need two or more
custom subnetworks here.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:49.980
It also means the
SQL client needs

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.640
to support something called the
multi-subnet failover option

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:54.846
in the connection string.

00:12:54.846 --> 00:12:56.220
So when you build
your operation,

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.190
you specify the connection
string to connect to SQL Server

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:00.200
Database, right?

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.500
That's the option
you need to specify.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.560
It means when you
[INAUDIBLE], usually you

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.530
need to run like an
ADO 4.5 or newer.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:12.750
Because this is billed
as like a faster failover

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.660
time, in our internal
testing, we usually

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.780
can be finishing
this whole failover

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:19.430
in the 10 to 15 seconds.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:25.430
Let's talk a little
bit about IP address.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.680
Cloud network usually
is very different

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:33.090
from a traditional on
prem network we've seen.

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:39.030
On a high level, TCE networking
is a big Layer 3 switch.

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:44.040
The usual features
like DHCP broadcast up

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.720
does not really
work the same way

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:50.170
as you expected in the
traditional network.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:54.090
The GCE network doesn't
know about the IP address,

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:56.830
the virtual IP you
config-- like a listen IP,

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.110
cluster IP you configure
in the cluster.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:04.750
So we are not automatically
sign this IP address to your VM.

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:08.650
That's why we need to switch
from DHCP to static IP

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:11.470
and also use routing
to configure this IP

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:13.550
address so the packet
can be forwarded

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:17.850
to the correct secure instance.

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:21.400
As we configure
SQL server, the TCE

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:25.740
advanced routing for the
cluster IP and the listen IP,

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:30.000
by the TCE routing
definition, the IP address

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.230
has to be outside of the varied
TCP subnetwork address range

00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:38.930
because this user
defined routing is--

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.150
if it's inside the
range, the routings

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:43.310
are already pretty fine.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.340
But however, from a
guest perspective,

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.860
this list IP has to
be in the same subnet

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:54.560
as the primary internal
IP from VM perspective.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.520
I know there's a little
bit of confusion.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:57.835
