00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:27.810
Now let's talk about the backup
and the disaster recovery.

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:31.500
There's a lot of different
options we can choose here.

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:34.170
First one you can continue
to use building the SQL

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:36.140
Server back up recoverability.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:40.060
You can use the SQL Server
manual studio, [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:40.060 --> 00:33:44.370
or PowerShell commandlet
to do the backup.

00:33:44.370 --> 00:33:47.430
Second you can leverage
always on availability group

00:33:47.430 --> 00:33:51.270
to have multiple replicas
ideally across different zones

00:33:51.270 --> 00:33:54.550
or even different regions.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:57.150
If one zone fails, the
service can just automatically

00:33:57.150 --> 00:33:59.930
failover to a different zone.

00:33:59.930 --> 00:34:03.070
Log shipping is an option.

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:06.240
The last option I want to point
out here is you can use PD--

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:07.500
persisent disk--

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:10.110
snapshot integrated with
the Windows Volume Shadow

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:11.800
Copy service.

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:16.320
It will back up your database
with application consistent

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:21.150
and make sure your services keep
running with out interrupted.

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:23.520
If you want to know more
about disaster recovery,

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:26.199
I want to point you to
the white paper here.

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:30.010
It's written by our
famous SQL Server expert

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:32.730
in [INAUDIBLE] in this
SQL Server community

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:34.710
and [INAUDIBLE] is also here.

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:39.000
You're welcome to talk
with him at the talk.

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:42.550
This white paper discussed
the disaster recovery

00:34:42.550 --> 00:34:44.760
for the SQL server on
TCE in great detail.

00:34:48.429 --> 00:34:52.469
I mentioned multiple [INAUDIBLE]
PD Snapshot with the VSS

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:57.290
integration is something very
powerful and useful for running

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:59.350
your SQL Server
on Google engine.

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:00.900
So why is that?

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:02.490
So what's VSS?

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:07.120
VSS Is technology included
in Microsoft Windows which

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:10.780
allows you take a manual or
automatic backup for the volume

00:35:10.780 --> 00:35:14.110
even when they're in use.

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:18.910
Result of VSS integration,
the persistent disk snapshot

00:35:18.910 --> 00:35:22.030
is not aware of any
[INAUDIBLE] state.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:25.250
It's called crash consistent.

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:28.010
For running system,
it's just like you

00:35:28.010 --> 00:35:29.990
pulled the cable from the wall.

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:35.370
Whatever is left on the
disk is your snapshot.

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:39.820
That's why on the windows,
the often suggestion

00:35:39.820 --> 00:35:43.660
to do a snapshot is
turn off your instance

00:35:43.660 --> 00:35:45.590
before you take a snapshot.

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:48.940
This is less than ideal.

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:51.560
For a typical operation,
the VSS requester

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:55.390
here will send a request
to the VSS service.

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:57.400
And the VSS service
will send a notification

00:35:57.400 --> 00:36:00.350
to all the VSS writers
[INAUDIBLE] in the system,

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:03.910
including the SQL Server
VSS's writer to tell you, hey,

00:36:03.910 --> 00:36:06.200
I'm going to take a snapshot.

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:08.210
Please get yourself ready.

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:10.420
So all the VSS writer,
we are starting--

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:11.800
like, a SQL Server
VSS writer, we

00:36:11.800 --> 00:36:15.260
are starting to coordinate
its internal state.

00:36:15.260 --> 00:36:17.770
For example, you need to
flush the internal cache

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:21.370
to get itself into a
self-consistent state.

00:36:21.370 --> 00:36:26.900
After everybody replied yes
to the VSS service, VSS--

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:28.090
no, everybody is ready.

00:36:28.090 --> 00:36:30.370
So he will float
in the I/O and then

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:33.137
send the request to
the VSS writer saying

00:36:33.137 --> 00:36:34.095
please take a snapshot.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.240
The workflow showed us that's
why we can take an application

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:44.990
consistent snapshot because
the storage system is

00:36:44.990 --> 00:36:48.250
working with the
application like SQL Server

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:50.610
to do the backup.

00:36:50.610 --> 00:36:54.800
By integrating PD snapshot
with the Windows VSS,

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:59.390
you combine the benefit
of super PD snapshot

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:03.850
with the consistency
guarantee from the VSS.

00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:06.790
You get all this without
affecting your running

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:08.040
instance.

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:11.050
It's the ideal tool to
do the SQL Server backup.

00:37:15.232 --> 00:37:16.690
Let me talk about
a little bit more

00:37:16.690 --> 00:37:21.410
for the other internal component
involved with the PD VSS stuff.

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:24.980
Internally, we have a
Google VSS agent running

00:37:24.980 --> 00:37:27.650
and we also have a
Google VSS provider

00:37:27.650 --> 00:37:32.620
in every Windows VM instance
in the Google Compute Engine.

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:36.030
On the back end, when a
persistent disks receives

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:42.540
a request to take a snapshot,
[INAUDIBLE] the hypervisor

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:45.980
will use an extended [INAUDIBLE]
protocol to send the signal

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:49.070
to the guest video driver.

00:37:49.070 --> 00:37:53.100
The video driver will
wake up the VSS agent.

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:56.600
VSS agent now acts just
like the requester.

00:37:56.600 --> 00:37:59.780
He will notify the
VSS service to say

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:02.210
I want to take a snapshot.

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:04.910
Once VSS service
gets the confirmation

00:38:04.910 --> 00:38:07.695
from all the writer,
including SQL server,

00:38:07.695 --> 00:38:11.490
then VSS service will notify
our Google VSS provider

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:14.660
saying, please take a snapshot.

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:16.970
Our provider here will
not take a snapshot.

00:38:16.970 --> 00:38:18.960
Instead, he will just
sent a notification

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.740
to the VSS driver,
the SCSI driver,

00:38:21.740 --> 00:38:25.910
and also back end PD service
saying from this perspective,

00:38:25.910 --> 00:38:26.720
we are ready.

00:38:26.720 --> 00:38:28.700
We already called it
our internal state.

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:30.350
We are self-consistent.

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:33.910
So you can go ahead
with your snapshot.

00:38:33.910 --> 00:38:36.830
This is an overly simplified,
high level overview

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:39.500
for how the workflow
working together,

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:41.290
but you should be
get a good idea how

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:44.290
everything works together to
do a VSS enabled a snapshot.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:50.050
Well, the internal logic
is quite complicated

00:38:50.050 --> 00:38:54.870
to actually implement the
PD VSS [INAUDIBLE] snapshot.

00:38:54.870 --> 00:38:57.570
To use that, to take a
VSS enabled snapshot,

00:38:57.570 --> 00:39:00.320
it's actually very easy.

00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:03.890
We add a new parameter in
the existing disc snapshot

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:07.130
API called a [INAUDIBLE] flash.

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:09.500
Depends on this parameter
is true or false,

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:12.180
a PD snapshot
workflow will decide

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:15.200
whether or not to involve
all these guest components

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:17.580
to finish the snapshot.

00:39:17.580 --> 00:39:19.930
By default, these
flag, this flash flag,

00:39:19.930 --> 00:39:24.590
is false unless you
specify it explicitly.

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:28.097
As with any other GCP feature,
you have redefined the way

00:39:28.097 --> 00:39:28.930
to use this feature.

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.390
You can use the
API directory, you

00:39:31.390 --> 00:39:33.670
can use GCloud
command line, and then

00:39:33.670 --> 00:39:35.140
you can also use cloud console.

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:43.285
As always, performance
tuning is always

00:39:43.285 --> 00:39:47.020
an important part of the
topic for the SQL Server.

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:49.340
After all, it's still
SQL Server no matter

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:51.070
if it's GCE or not GCE.

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:53.290
So all your existing
knowledge for the performance

00:39:53.290 --> 00:39:55.385
tuning for the SQL
Server, most of that

00:39:55.385 --> 00:39:57.280
is still applicable here.

00:39:57.280 --> 00:40:02.060
I want to point out to
some difference on the GCE.

00:40:02.060 --> 00:40:04.480
First, there's a
different storage option

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:08.810
on GCE-- local
SSD versus PD SSD.

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:12.140
So which storage
option should I use?

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:16.330
Local SSD is faster, much
faster than the PD SSD,

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:18.880
but it's not as
durable as PD SSD.

00:40:18.880 --> 00:40:21.650
In general, for those
database workload,

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:25.490
we suggest you go
with the PD SSD.

00:40:25.490 --> 00:40:27.830
If you want to go
with local SD, you

00:40:27.830 --> 00:40:30.950
need to configure using
always on [INAUDIBLE] group,

00:40:30.950 --> 00:40:36.300
configure multiple replicas to
just prepare for the failure.

00:40:36.300 --> 00:40:40.982
However, local SD is very useful
to put up your performance.

00:40:40.982 --> 00:40:43.340
A couple of scenario here--
the first scenario here, you

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:47.000
can use local SSD to hold
onto your Windows page

00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:51.590
file to speed up the page
for the handling for Windows.

00:40:51.590 --> 00:40:53.330
And then you can also
use the local SSD

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:54.530
to hold the temp DB file.

00:40:58.160 --> 00:41:00.050
Generally speaking,
it's a good idea

00:41:00.050 --> 00:41:05.160
to have-- you have a dedicated
disk to host all your database.

00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:09.180
So when you format your
disk, format the file system

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:13.830
on the disk, we suggest you to
set the allocation unit size

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:16.950
as 32K because this will work
best with the underlying PD

00:41:16.950 --> 00:41:20.350
infrastructure.

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:22.176
On the on-prem
environment, usually we

00:41:22.176 --> 00:41:23.050
heard the suggestion.

00:41:23.050 --> 00:41:26.320
I get, hey, keep your
data file and the log

00:41:26.320 --> 00:41:31.790
file separate on different disks
for the isolated performance.

00:41:31.790 --> 00:41:34.070
But however, on
cloud environment,

00:41:34.070 --> 00:41:37.250
the persistent disk is
already distributed disk.

00:41:37.250 --> 00:41:39.420
[INAUDIBLE] back
up a lot of disk

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:41.000
on the machine on the back.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:47.070
So keep the data in the
log in the same large PD.

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:49.490
We will not lose any
performance and you'll

00:41:49.490 --> 00:41:52.080
make your life a lot
easier when you deal

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:53.340
with the things like backup.

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:59.810
Again, this is a very
high level summary.

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:02.460
You were interested, you
